{
 "block": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5",
 "edits": [
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-001",
   "finding": "report #40",
   "old": "Silica absorbs strongly near 9–10 µm, because the Si–O bonds of glass vibrate at those frequencies and soak up the radiation (Si–O vibrational absorption).",
   "new": "Silica-based glass absorbs throughout 8–14 µm. The Si–O stretching vibration absorbs strongly near 9–10 µm and a second Si–O–Si vibration near 12.5 µm, and the broad wings of these bands, together with multiphonon absorption, fill the rest of the band, so a windshield several millimetres thick transmits essentially nothing there.",
   "why": "The heading claims opacity across 8–14 µm but the bullet explained only 9–10 µm and repeated itself in brackets."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-002",
   "finding": "report §3 flag (f-number and IFOV notation)",
   "old": "Its instantaneous field of view (IFOV), the angle seen by one pixel,",
   "new": "Its instantaneous field of view (IFOV, written $\\theta_{\\rm pix}$ in §C0), the angle seen by one pixel,",
   "why": "Links Part A's IFOV to the primer's symbol for the same quantity."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-003",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (pedestrian 0.5 m × 1.7 m); report #6",
   "old": "$d_c=\\sqrt{1.8\\times0.5}\\approx0.95$ m. Here 1.8 m is the assumed height and 0.5 m the assumed width,",
   "new": "$d_c=\\sqrt{1.7\\times0.5}\\approx0.92$ m. Here 1.7 m is the assumed height and 0.5 m the assumed width (the pedestrian size used throughout this report),",
   "why": "Shared input: the pedestrian is 0.5 m × 1.7 m everywhere; sqrt(0.85) = 0.92 m."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-004",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (pedestrian 0.5 m × 1.7 m); report #6",
   "old": "\\frac{0.95}{1.5\\times1.31\\times10^{-3}}\\approx480\\ \\text{m},\\qquad R_\\text{rec}\\approx\\frac{0.95}{6\\times1.31\\times10^{-3}}\\approx120\\ \\text{m} \\tag{3}$$\n\n- These are **angular-sampling ceilings** (roughly 480 m for detection, 120 m for recognition)",
   "new": "\\frac{0.92}{1.5\\times1.31\\times10^{-3}}\\approx470\\ \\text{m},\\qquad R_\\text{rec}\\approx\\frac{0.92}{6\\times1.31\\times10^{-3}}\\approx120\\ \\text{m} \\tag{3}$$\n\n- These are **angular-sampling ceilings** (roughly 470 m for detection, 120 m for recognition)",
   "why": "Recomputed with d_c = 0.92 m: 0.92/(1.5×1.31e-3) = 468 ≈ 470 m; 0.92/(6×1.31e-3) = 117 ≈ 120 m (two significant figures, as before)."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-005",
   "finding": "report #17; report §3 flag (spelling); report §1 Problem 3 (tags)",
   "old": "Valeo + Teledyne FLIR ASIL-B program (first contract late 2023). ASIL (automotive safety integrity level) is the ISO 26262 functional-safety rating, from A (lowest) to D (highest), that a part must carry before a safety function such as braking may rely on it.\n- Thermal cameras built on uncooled microbolometers (the usual thermal sensor, an array of tiny resistors warmed by the absorbed infrared) are dual-use controlled, i.e. export-controlled because they also have military uses, when they run faster than 9 Hz (US export classification ECCN 6A003.b.4.b; the bare detector array is ECCN 6A002.a.3.f at any frame rate) [P]. Note 3.c to 6A003.b.4.b releases cameras specially designed for civilian passenger vehicles under 4,500 kg gross vehicle weight, placed solely to assist the driver, and built to stop working when removed from their vehicle. Such cameras are ECCN 6A993.a, controlled for anti-terrorism reasons only, at any frame rate. Teledyne FLIR classifies its Tura (640 × 512, up to 60 Hz) as 6A993.a with its authentication feature and 6A003.b.4.b without it [P].",
   "new": "Valeo + Teledyne FLIR ASIL-B programme (first contract late 2023) [P]. ASIL (automotive safety integrity level) is the ISO 26262 functional-safety rating, from A (lowest) to D (highest), that a part must carry before a safety function such as braking may rely on it.\n- **Export controls.** In the US, a thermal camera built on an uncooled microbolometer (the usual thermal sensor, an array of tiny resistors warmed by the absorbed infrared) is a dual-use item, one with military as well as civil uses, and is export-controlled if it runs faster than 9 Hz. Its Export Control Classification Number (ECCN) is then 6A003.b.4.b; the bare detector array is ECCN 6A002.a.3.f at any frame rate [P].\n  - Note 3.c to 6A003.b.4.b excludes from that entry cameras specially designed for civilian passenger vehicles under 4,500 kg gross vehicle weight, placed solely to assist the driver and built to stop working when removed from their vehicle. Such cameras fall under ECCN 6A993.a, which is controlled for anti-terrorism reasons only, at any frame rate [P].\n  - Teledyne FLIR, for example, classifies its Tura camera (640 × 512 pixels, up to 60 Hz) as 6A993.a with its authentication feature, which ties the camera to its vehicle, and as 6A003.b.4.b without it [P].\n  - An automotive thermal camera that runs faster than 9 Hz therefore needs this vehicle lock to escape the stricter control, a design and validation item that a visible-light camera does not need [E].",
   "why": "Splits a five-sentence bullet, expands ECCN, replaces jargon and states why export control weighs on thermal; [P] from the Valeo release in Appendix 2."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-006",
   "finding": "report §3 flag (to verify, line 305)",
   "old": "The 73 km/h operating threshold is therefore not a tested avoidance speed under lower beams.",
   "new": "The 73 km/h operating threshold is therefore not a tested avoidance speed in any of the night runs, with either beam.",
   "why": "The speed caps quoted cover all listed night runs, upper and lower beam, not only lower beams."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-007",
   "finding": "report §1 Problem 3 (tags); report §3 flag (spelling)",
   "old": "broadens crash-avoidance scenarios and includes low-light testing. Euro NCAP and C-NCAP are the European and Chinese new-car assessment programs, which award consumer safety stars. Euro NCAP's official 2026 announcement does **not** establish a new general rain/fog test for pedestrian AEB.\n- **C-NCAP 2027** explicitly adds adverse-weather (rain/fog) simulations and a night two-wheeler scenario.",
   "new": "broadens crash-avoidance scenarios and includes low-light testing [P]. Euro NCAP and C-NCAP are the European and Chinese new-car assessment programmes, which award consumer safety stars. Euro NCAP's official 2026 announcement does **not** establish a new general rain/fog test for pedestrian AEB [P].\n- **C-NCAP 2027** explicitly adds adverse-weather (rain/fog) simulations and a night two-wheeler scenario [P].",
   "why": "Untagged facts sourced to the official Euro NCAP and C-NCAP pages listed in the text and Appendix 2; British spelling."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-008",
   "finding": "report #39; report §3 flag (f-number and IFOV notation)",
   "old": "- **Thermal as an AEB input rather than a display:** Zeekr 9X (Raytron); Valeo + Teledyne FLIR ASIL-B (\"leading global OEM\", late 2023; ASIL-B is the second of the four ISO 26262 safety-integrity levels); Magna fifth generation (late 2025, shrinking from golf-ball to sugar-cube size).\n- **Cheaper Chinese sensors and optics:** Raytron, Guide, 8 µm pixels. Smaller pixels shrink the lens, and the optics are expensive (§A3, item 6). At fixed IFOV (the angle seen by one pixel) and fixed f-number, lens area scales with pixel pitch as $p^2$, where $p$ is the pixel pitch (centre-to-centre spacing). The argument has three steps:\n  - a fixed IFOV $=p/f$ forces the focal length $f$ to scale as $f\\propto p$;\n  - the aperture diameter $D=f/N$ at a fixed f-number $N$ (focal length divided by aperture diameter)",
   "new": "- **Thermal as an AEB input rather than a display.** The Zeekr 9X uses a Raytron thermal camera (640×512) as an input to automatic emergency braking, claimed as the \"world's first thermal AEB\" [P] (§A1). In late 2023 Valeo and Teledyne FLIR announced a first contract to supply an ASIL-B thermal camera to a \"leading global OEM\", an unnamed carmaker [P]; ASIL-B is the second-lowest of the four ISO 26262 levels (§A3, item 6). Magna's fifth generation (late 2025) shrinks the camera from golf-ball to sugar-cube size.\n- **Cheaper Chinese sensors and optics.** Chinese makers such as Raytron and Guide (Wuhan Guide Infrared) offer cheaper sensors and optics, and pixel pitch has fallen to 8 µm. Smaller pixels shrink the lens, and the optics are expensive (§A3, item 6). At fixed IFOV (the angle seen by one pixel) and fixed f-number, lens area scales with pixel pitch as $p^2$, where $p$ is the pixel pitch (centre-to-centre spacing). The argument has three steps:\n  - a fixed IFOV $=p/f$ forces the focal length $f$ to scale as $f\\propto p$;\n  - the aperture diameter $D=f/F_\\#$ at a fixed f-number $F_\\#$ (focal length divided by aperture diameter)",
   "why": "Telegraphic notes become sentences; OEM and 'second' clarified; [P] from the Raytron and Valeo releases in Appendix 2; f-number symbol as in §C0."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-009",
   "finding": "report #19",
   "old": "while thermal does not see colour at all.",
   "new": "whereas thermal is insensitive to clothing colour, because it senses emitted heat rather than reflected light.",
   "why": "'Does not see colour' read as a weakness; the point is insensitivity to clothing colour."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-010",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (pedestrian 0.5 m × 1.7 m; δθ for point spacing); report #6; report #18",
   "old": "a 0.5×1.8 m pedestrian gets about **30 points at 100 m and about 7 at 200 m**. At 0.05°, it gets about 118 and 30 points [E]. The count is the target area divided by the area of one grid cell:\n\n$$N_{\\rm pts}\\approx\\frac{w_{\\rm ped}\\,h_{\\rm ped}}{(R\\theta)^2} \\tag{4}$$\n\n  Here $N_{\\rm pts}$ is the number of lidar points on the pedestrian, $w_{\\rm ped}=0.5$ m and $h_{\\rm ped}=1.8$ m are the pedestrian's assumed width and height (area 0.9 m²), $R$ is the range in metres and $\\theta$ the angular step of the sampling grid in radians, so that $R\\theta$ is the side of one grid cell at range $R$. The equation assumes a uniform square grid, small angles, and a return from every grid direction that falls on the body (no dropouts or occlusion).\n- At 0.1° ($\\theta=1.745\\times10^{-3}$ rad) and 100 m a cell is 0.175 m on a side, so $N_{\\rm pts}\\approx0.9\\ {\\rm m^2}/(0.175\\ {\\rm m})^2\\approx30$; at 200 m, $N_{\\rm pts}\\approx0.9/0.349^2\\approx7$. Halving the angle quadruples the count: $4\\times29.5\\approx118$ at 100 m and $4\\times7.4\\approx30$ at 200 m. At 200 m and 0.1°, then, a pedestrian is only a handful of points.",
   "new": "a 0.5×1.7 m pedestrian gets about **28 points at 100 m and about 7 at 200 m**. At 0.05°, it gets about 112 and 28 points [E]. The count is the target area divided by the area of one grid cell:\n\n$$N_{\\rm pts}\\approx\\frac{w_{\\rm ped}\\,h_{\\rm ped}}{(R\\,\\delta\\theta)^2} \\tag{4}$$\n\n  Here $N_{\\rm pts}$ is the number of lidar points on the pedestrian, $w_{\\rm ped}=0.5$ m and $h_{\\rm ped}=1.7$ m are the pedestrian's assumed width and height (area 0.85 m²; the size used throughout this report), $R$ is the range in metres and $\\delta\\theta$ the angular step of the sampling grid in radians, so that $R\\,\\delta\\theta$ is the side of one grid cell at range $R$. The equation assumes a uniform square grid, small angles, and a return from every grid direction that falls on the body (no dropouts or occlusion).\n- At 0.1° ($\\delta\\theta=1.745\\times10^{-3}$ rad) and 100 m a cell is $100\\times1.745\\times10^{-3}\\approx0.1745$ m on a side, so $N_{\\rm pts}\\approx0.85\\ {\\rm m^2}/(0.1745\\ {\\rm m})^2\\approx27.9$, about 28; at 200 m the cell is 0.349 m and $N_{\\rm pts}\\approx0.85/0.349^2\\approx7.0$. Halving the angle quadruples the count: $4\\times27.9\\approx112$ at 100 m and $4\\times7.0\\approx28$ at 200 m. At 200 m and 0.1°, then, a pedestrian is only a handful of points.\n- For comparison, the 320×240 thermal camera of §A3 (IFOV 1.31 mrad, Eq. (1)) has a pixel footprint of $R\\cdot\\text{IFOV}=100\\times1.31\\times10^{-3}\\approx0.131$ m at 100 m and 0.262 m at 200 m. Eq. (4) with the IFOV in place of $\\delta\\theta$ then puts the pedestrian on about $0.85/0.131^2\\approx50$ pixels at 100 m and $0.85/0.262^2\\approx12$ at 200 m [E]. A 640×512 sensor over the same 24° horizontal field halves the IFOV and so quadruples these counts. Even a low-resolution thermal camera therefore samples the pedestrian more densely than a 0.1° lidar (28 and 7 points).",
   "why": "Counts recomputed for 0.85 m² (27.9, 7.0, 112, 28); grid step written δθ; adds the thermal pixel count the item heading promises (50 and 12 pixels)."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-011",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (R_max); report §3 flags (dark-clothing reflectance; pointers to add)",
   "old": "setting $P_{\\rm rx}$ equal to the threshold gives $R_\\text{max}\\propto\\sqrt{\\rho}$, where $R_\\text{max}$ is the maximum detection range.\n- Going from white ($\\rho\\approx0.8$) to dark ($\\rho\\approx0.1$) cuts range to about $0.35\\times$: at a fixed detection threshold, $R_\\text{max}\\propto\\sqrt{\\rho}$ gives $\\sqrt{0.1/0.8}=\\sqrt{0.125}\\approx0.35$. Dark clothing therefore costs a lidar about two-thirds of its white-target range.\n- The scaling holds only under the assumptions of Eq. (5). Glossy surfaces and retroreflectors are not Lambertian and send far more or far less light back toward the sensor, depending on angle. A target smaller than the beam footprint returns power that falls faster than $1/R^2$, which changes the scaling.",
   "new": "setting $P_{\\rm rx}$ equal to the threshold gives $R_{\\max}\\propto\\sqrt{\\rho}$, where $R_{\\max}$ is the maximum detection range. Eq. (5) is the proportional form of the full photon budget, Eq. (23) in §C0 subsection 1.\n- At the lidar's near-infrared wavelength, going from white ($\\rho\\approx0.8$) to dark clothing ($\\rho\\approx0.1$) cuts range to about $0.35\\times$: at a fixed detection threshold, $R_{\\max}\\propto\\sqrt{\\rho}$ gives $\\sqrt{0.1/0.8}=\\sqrt{0.125}\\approx0.35$. Dark clothing therefore costs a lidar about two-thirds of its white-target range.\n- The scaling holds only under the assumptions of Eq. (5). Glossy surfaces and retroreflectors are not Lambertian and send far more or far less light back toward the sensor, depending on angle. A target smaller than the beam footprint returns power that falls as $1/R^4$ rather than $1/R^2$ (§C0 subsection 1, after Eq. (23)), which changes the scaling.",
   "why": "R_max written as elsewhere in the report; reflectance tied to its wavelength; Eq. (5) and the small-target case point to §C0."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-012",
   "finding": "report #20",
   "old": "Mie theory, the exact theory of light scattering by a sphere, gives $Q_\\text{ext}$ as a function of $x$.\n  - At 905 nm, fine fog droplets ($r\\approx2$ µm) give $x=2\\pi\\times2/0.905\\approx14$ and $Q_\\text{ext}\\approx2$ (the large-particle limit of Mie extinction).\n  - At 10 µm, $x=2\\pi\\times2/10\\approx1.3$, well below the extinction peak.",
   "new": "Mie theory, the exact theory of light scattering by a sphere, gives $Q_\\text{ext}$ as a function of $x$ and of the droplet's complex refractive index $n+i\\kappa$, whose real part $n$ is the ordinary refractive index and whose imaginary part $\\kappa$, the extinction index, sets absorption.\n  - At 905 nm, fine fog droplets ($r\\approx2$ µm) give $x=2\\pi\\times2/0.905\\approx14$ and $Q_\\text{ext}\\approx2$, the large-particle limit of Mie extinction; a full Mie calculation for water gives ≈2.25 at this size [E].\n  - At 10 µm, $x=2\\pi\\times2/10\\approx1.3$, well below the extinction peak. A Mie calculation with the tabulated $n+i\\kappa\\approx1.22+0.05i$ of liquid water at 10 µm gives $Q_\\text{ext}\\approx0.26$ [E].\n  - A fog of $N_{\\rm d}$ droplets per cubic metre, all of radius $r$, has $\\alpha=N_{\\rm d}\\,\\pi r^2Q_\\text{ext}$. In the same fine-droplet fog, $\\alpha$ is therefore about nine times smaller at 10 µm than at 905 nm ($2.25/0.26\\approx8.7$) [E].\n  - For large droplets ($r=10$ µm), $x=2\\pi\\times10/10\\approx6.3$ at 10 µm and $Q_\\text{ext}\\approx2.6$, no smaller than the ≈2 at 905 nm, so the advantage disappears [E].",
   "why": "Quantifies the 10 µm case and links Q_ext to alpha; values checked with an independent Bohren-Huffman Mie calculation (0.26, 2.25, 2.55-2.58)."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-013",
   "finding": "report #21",
   "old": "- **A water film** on the thermal window is opaque: the absorption length at 10 µm is about 10–20 µm. The absorption length $1/\\alpha_{\\rm w}$ is the depth over which the intensity falls by a factor $e$. Here $\\alpha_{\\rm w}=4\\pi\\kappa/\\lambda$ is the intensity absorption coefficient of liquid water, and $\\kappa$ is the extinction index, the imaginary part of water's complex refractive index $n+i\\kappa$ (whose real part $n$ is the ordinary refractive index) [E: the absorption length is $1/\\alpha_{\\rm w}=\\lambda/(4\\pi\\kappa)$. Assuming the tabulated imaginary refractive index of liquid water, $\\kappa\\approx0.05$ at 10 µm, $1/\\alpha_{\\rm w}\\approx10^{-5}\\ {\\rm m}/(4\\pi\\times0.05)\\approx1.6\\times10^{-5}$ m, about 16 µm; $\\kappa\\approx0.04$–0.1 across 9–11 µm gives about 9–18 µm]. Because this length is so short, even a thin film of water blinds the camera.",
   "new": "- **A water film** on the thermal window is opaque. The intensity absorption coefficient of liquid water is $\\alpha_{\\rm w}=4\\pi\\kappa/\\lambda$, with $\\kappa$ the extinction index defined above, and its inverse, the absorption length $1/\\alpha_{\\rm w}$, is the depth over which the intensity falls by a factor $e$. With the tabulated $\\kappa\\approx0.05$ at 10 µm, $1/\\alpha_{\\rm w}=\\lambda/(4\\pi\\kappa)=10^{-5}\\ {\\rm m}/(4\\pi\\times0.05)\\approx1.6\\times10^{-5}$ m, about 16 µm; across 9–11 µm, where $\\kappa\\approx0.04$–0.1, it is about 9–18 µm [E]. A 50 µm film therefore transmits only about $e^{-50/16}\\approx4\\%$ at 10 µm [E], so even a thin film of water blinds the camera.",
   "why": "Derivation moved out of the tag and stated before its use; the result (16 µm, 9–18 µm) replaces the mismatched 10–20 µm; adds the film transmission."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-014",
   "finding": "report #7; report #16; report §3 flag (spelling)",
   "old": "so the 50–100 m figures below are representative, not a universal limit. \"Better headlights\" help in three ways:\n1. getting real-world low beams closer to that ceiling (pattern, aim, leveling, curve lighting);\n2. **delivering high-beam-level light more of the time without relying on the driver**, through automatic high beam (which switches between low and high beam by itself) and adaptive driving beam (ADB, which keeps the high beam on but darkens the parts of it that would dazzle other road users);\n3. aiming light at hazards that sensors have already detected (marking or spot lights).\n\nLever 2 has a large photometric opportunity",
   "new": "so the figures below (the cutoff meets the road 50–100 m ahead, depending on aim, and the usable reach for dark targets is about 50–80 m) are representative, not a universal limit. \"Better headlights\" help through three levers:\n- lever (a), getting real-world low beams closer to their glare-limited ceiling (pattern, aim, levelling, curve lighting);\n- lever (b), **delivering high-beam-level light more of the time without relying on the driver**, through automatic high beam (which switches between low and high beam by itself) and adaptive driving beam (ADB, which keeps the high beam on but darkens the parts of it that would dazzle other road users);\n- lever (c), aiming light at hazards that sensors have already detected (marking or spot lights).\n\nLever (b) has a large photometric opportunity",
   "why": "Separates cutoff distance (50–100 m) from usable reach (50–80 m); lettered levers no longer clash with §A6.3's numbered items."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-015",
   "finding": "report §3 flag (evidence tags, B50L)",
   "old": "must stay within 50–350 cd [T].",
   "new": "must stay within 50–350 cd [S, via *Scientific Reports* 2026, which gives it for ECE R123].",
   "why": "Appendix 2's source is a journal paper quoting the regulation, which the legend tags [S], not trade press."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-016",
   "finding": "report #11; report §1 Problem 3 (table lead-in)",
   "old": "which means that the extended cutoff plane has passed below the road surface.\n\nFor $h=0.65$ m:",
   "new": "which means that the extended cutoff plane has passed below the road surface. Eqs. (6)–(7) and the table below describe the flat, oncoming-side part of the cutoff. On the kerb side the 15° rise lifts the cutoff, so for a pedestrian at the kerb-side road edge the reach is set mainly by the beam's intensity in that direction (Eq. (8) and the photometry table below) rather than by this geometry.\n\nFor a lamp mounted at $h=0.65$ m, Eq. (6) gives the distances below at which the cutoff meets the road:",
   "why": "Says which part of the cutoff the geometry describes, and gives the aim table a lead-in sentence."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-017",
   "finding": "report #11",
   "old": "- Beyond $d$, a standing pedestrian's whole body lies above the cutoff",
   "new": "- Beyond $d$, a standing pedestrian in the flat part of the pattern lies wholly above the cutoff",
   "why": "Restricts the claim to the flat part of the cutoff that Eqs. (6)–(7) describe."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-018",
   "finding": "report #38; report §3 flag (dark-clothing reflectance)",
   "old": "- A dark pedestrian ($\\rho\\approx0.05$–0.1) at 5 lux has $L=\\rho E/\\pi\\approx0.08$–0.16 cd/m² ($0.05\\times5/\\pi\\approx0.08$ and $0.1\\times5/\\pi\\approx0.16$), which is mesopic vision. Mesopic vision is the twilight regime in which both the eye's rods and cones work, and contrast sensitivity, acuity and colour vision are reduced.\n- In the glare-limited zone the illuminance at 50 m is $E=350\\ {\\rm cd}/(50\\ {\\rm m})^2=0.14$ lux, so $L\\approx0.05\\times0.14/\\pi\\approx0.002$ to $0.1\\times0.14/\\pi\\approx0.004$ cd/m², effectively invisible.",
   "new": "- A dark pedestrian ($\\rho\\approx0.05$–0.1 in visible light) at 5 lux has $L=\\rho E/\\pi\\approx0.08$–0.16 cd/m² ($0.05\\times5/\\pi\\approx0.08$ and $0.1\\times5/\\pi\\approx0.16$), which lies in the mesopic range of luminance (roughly 0.005–5 cd/m² [U]). Mesopic vision is the twilight regime in which both the eye's rods and cones work, and contrast sensitivity, acuity and colour vision are reduced.\n- In the glare-limited zone the illuminance at 50 m is $E=350\\ {\\rm cd}/(50\\ {\\rm m})^2=0.14$ lux, so $L\\approx0.05\\times0.14/\\pi\\approx0.002$ to $0.1\\times0.14/\\pi\\approx0.004$ cd/m². That is below the lower edge of the mesopic range and, to an eye adapted to the road lit by your own low beam, effectively invisible.",
   "why": "A luminance is not 'vision'; ties both values to the mesopic range and states the reflectance's wavelength."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-019",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (shared inputs: 0.7 g against 5–6 m/s²)",
   "old": "a firm stop on a dry, level road, with no allowance for the brakes to build up.",
   "new": "a firm stop on a dry, level road, with no allowance for the brakes to build up. Part E uses a gentler 5–6 m/s², down to the 5 m/s² that UN R157 assumes for planning (§E2).",
   "why": "Keeps the two decelerations apart and says why they differ."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-020",
   "finding": "report #12",
   "old": "light). Setting Eq. (11) equal to the reach $d_{\\rm reach}$, i.e. $v\\,t_r+v^2/(2a_{\\rm b})=d_{\\rm reach}$, with $t_r=1.5$ s and $a_{\\rm b}=0.7g$, and solving for $v$, stopping distance reaches 50 m at about 64 km/h, 65 m at about 77 km/h and 80 m at about 88 km/h [E].",
   "new": "light).\n- These speeds follow from setting Eq. (11) equal to a reach $d_{\\rm reach}$, $v\\,t_r+v^2/(2a_{\\rm b})=d_{\\rm reach}$, and solving this quadratic for the speed: $v=a_{\\rm b}\\left[\\sqrt{t_r^2+2d_{\\rm reach}/a_{\\rm b}}-t_r\\right]$, with $t_r=1.5$ s and $a_{\\rm b}=6.87$ m/s². For $d_{\\rm reach}=50$ m, $v=6.87\\times(\\sqrt{2.25+14.56}-1.5)\\approx17.9$ m/s $\\approx64$ km/h. For 65 m, $v=6.87\\times(\\sqrt{2.25+18.92}-1.5)\\approx21.3$ m/s $\\approx77$ km/h, and for 80 m, $v=6.87\\times(\\sqrt{2.25+23.29}-1.5)\\approx24.4$ m/s $\\approx88$ km/h [E].",
   "why": "Splits a 55-word sentence into its own bullet and shows the quadratic solution with substitutions."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-021",
   "finding": "report #10; report §3 flags (pointers to add; evidence tags, J599; spelling)",
   "old": "This is the same $Z^2/h$ geometry as monocular camera depth: a camera at height $h$ that judges the distance $Z$ to a point on the road from its angle below the horizon has an error that grows as $Z^2/h$ times its angle error. For example, at the 0.57° aim ($d\\approx65$ m), an aim error of 0.1° ($1.75\\times10^{-3}$ rad) moves the cutoff by about $65^2\\times1.75\\times10^{-3}/0.65\\approx11$ m [E].\n- SAE J599 (the SAE recommended practice for headlamp-aim inspection) allows a vertical aim of 0 ± 0.76° (below 90 cm mounting height) [P, via IIHS].\n- IIHS measured factory aims on new cars between about −0.5° and +0.4° [I].\n- IIHS notes that a 0.25° downward aim already drops the cutoff below its 25 cm sensor beyond 100 m. With the 0.65 m lamp height above, Eq. (7) gives a cutoff height at 100 m of $0.65-100\\times\\tan0.25^\\circ\\approx0.21$ m, and the cutoff crosses 25 cm at $(0.65-0.25)/\\tan0.25^\\circ\\approx92$ m [E].\n- Production aim, load and pitch leveling are therefore first-order variables, not details.",
   "new": "This is the same $Z^2/h$ geometry as monocular camera depth (§E2, Eq. (153)): a camera at height $h$ that judges the distance $Z$ to a point on the road from its angle below the horizon has an error that grows as $Z^2/h$ times its angle error. For example, at the 0.57° aim ($d\\approx65$ m), an aim error of 0.1° ($1.75\\times10^{-3}$ rad) moves the cutoff by about $65^2\\times1.75\\times10^{-3}/0.65\\approx11$ m [E].\n- SAE J599 (the SAE recommended practice for headlamp-aim inspection) allows a vertical aim of 0 ± 0.76° (below 90 cm mounting height) [S, via IIHS].\n- IIHS measured factory aims on new cars between about −0.5° and +0.4° [I].\n- IIHS notes that a 0.25° downward aim already drops the cutoff below the 25 cm height at which it measures illuminance (Photometry, above) beyond 100 m. With the 0.65 m lamp height above, Eq. (7) gives a cutoff height at 100 m of $0.65\\ {\\rm m}-100\\ {\\rm m}\\times\\tan0.25^\\circ\\approx0.21$ m, and the cutoff falls to the 0.25 m measurement height at $(0.65\\ {\\rm m}-0.25\\ {\\rm m})/\\tan0.25^\\circ\\approx92$ m [E].\n- Production aim, load and pitch levelling are therefore first-order variables, not details.",
   "why": "Units separate the 0.25 m height from the 0.25° angle; the 25 cm point is IIHS's measurement height; pointer to Eq. (153); J599 value via IIHS is [S]."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-022",
   "finding": "report §3 flag (spelling)",
   "old": "- **Aim and leveling:** tighter factory aim; automatic and dynamic leveling,",
   "new": "- **Aim and levelling:** tighter factory aim; automatic and dynamic levelling,",
   "why": "British spelling, as elsewhere in the report."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-023",
   "finding": "report #13; report #16",
   "old": "not a controlled estimate of ADB's effect. The rating also gives credit for automatic high-beam switching, so part of the observed benefit may reflect lever 2.",
   "new": "not a controlled estimate of what better low beams alone achieve. The rating also gives credit for automatic high-beam switching, so part of the observed benefit may reflect lever (b).",
   "why": "The IIHS evidence concerns headlight ratings, not ADB; lever label follows the short answer."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-024",
   "finding": "report #14",
   "old": "- So the binding constraint is **behavioural and social** (drivers dip their lights to avoid dazzling others), not photometric.",
   "new": "- So the binding constraint is **behavioural**, not photometric: drivers leave high beams off even when there is no one to dazzle (on unlit rural roads with no other traffic they used them for only 25.4% of the distance), as well as dipping them for other traffic.",
   "why": "The bracketed explanation contradicted the 25.4% use on empty roads just quoted."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-025",
   "finding": "report #16; report §1 Problem 3 (tags)",
   "old": "**The engineering answers are automation, not persuasion:**\n1. **Automatic high-beam switching.** A camera detects other vehicles' lamps and toggles the beam, removing the behavioural part.\n2. **Adaptive driving beam (ADB, matrix or pixel \"glare-free high beam\").** The beam is divided into many individually switched LED segments or pixels. It keeps high-beam intensity everywhere except dark \"tunnels\" around detected vehicles, removing most of the glare constraint.\n   - **Europe and China:** widespread since about 2013 (Audi Matrix LED, Mercedes Multibeam and Digital Light, BMW Selective Beam, and many Chinese makers).\n   - **United States:** legalized by NHTSA (the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration) in **Feb 2022** (FMVSS 108 amendment), but the vehicle-level track test is stricter than global practice.\n     - It requires a 1° transition zone, against about 4° elsewhere.\n     - Component minimums conflict with track-test maximums in right curves: a lamp that meets its component-level minimum intensities can exceed the glare maxima of the vehicle-level track test there.\n     - NHTSA **denied all 12 petitions for reconsideration** on 30 Dec 2024 [P].\n     - As of 2025, **Rivian was the only automaker offering fully compliant ADB in the US** [T].\n     - In practice, most US cars still switch between low and high beam.\n3. **Sensor-directed marking or spot light.** Light is aimed at detected pedestrians or animals: BMW Dynamic Light Spot (with thermal), Audi marker lights, and pixel or digital-micromirror (DMD, the chip of tiny tilting mirrors used in video projectors) headlights that can also project lane markings.",
   "new": "**The engineering answers are automation, not persuasion.** The first two below deliver lever (b) of the short answer, and the third is lever (c):\n- **Automatic high-beam switching.** A camera detects other vehicles' lamps and toggles the beam, removing the behavioural part.\n- **Adaptive driving beam (ADB, matrix or pixel \"glare-free high beam\").** The beam is divided into many individually switched LED segments or pixels. It keeps high-beam intensity everywhere except dark \"tunnels\" around detected vehicles, removing most of the glare constraint.\n  - **Europe and China:** widespread since about 2013 (Audi Matrix LED, Mercedes Multibeam and Digital Light, BMW Selective Beam, and many Chinese makers).\n  - **United States:** legalized by NHTSA (the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration) in **Feb 2022** (FMVSS 108 amendment) [P], but the vehicle-level track test is stricter than global practice.\n    - It requires a 1° transition zone, against about 4° elsewhere.\n    - Component minimums conflict with track-test maximums in right curves: a lamp that meets its component-level minimum intensities can exceed the glare maxima of the vehicle-level track test there.\n    - NHTSA **denied all 12 petitions for reconsideration** on 30 Dec 2024 [P].\n    - As of 2025, **Rivian was the only automaker offering fully compliant ADB in the US** [T].\n    - In practice, most US cars still switch between low and high beam.\n- **Sensor-directed marking or spot light.** Light is aimed at detected pedestrians or animals: BMW Dynamic Light Spot (with thermal), Audi marker lights, and pixel or digital-micromirror (DMD, the chip of tiny tilting mirrors used in video projectors) headlights that can also project lane markings.",
   "why": "Numbered items 1–3 no longer collide with the levers; maps them to levers (b) and (c); [P] from the NHTSA ADB rule in Appendix 2."
  },
  {
