  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-031",
   "finding": "report #31",
   "old": "- Wide baselines move the problem to calibration: about 35 µrad of relative orientation between the two cameras must stay stable across a flexing body (NODAR's core IP, i.e. intellectual property; NODAR is a wide-baseline stereo company). A relative rotation of the cameras shifts every disparity directly, so it eats into the same error budget as matching noise. That is the 0.25 px disparity budget of the 3840 px / 30° camera: one pixel subtends $1/f=1/7{,}166$ rad $\\approx140$ µrad (its IFOV, the instantaneous field of view of a single pixel), and $0.25\\times140\\approx35$ µrad.",
   "new": "- Wide baselines move the problem to calibration. A relative rotation of the two cameras shifts every disparity directly, so it eats into the same error budget as matching noise. For the 3840 px / 30° camera one pixel subtends $1/f=1/7{,}166$ rad $\\approx140$ µrad (its IFOV, the instantaneous field of view of a single pixel), so the 0.25 px disparity budget is $0.25\\times140\\approx35$ µrad: the cameras' relative orientation must stay stable to about 35 µrad while the body flexes. Holding it there is the core intellectual property (IP) claimed by NODAR, a wide-baseline stereo company [P].",
   "why": "Derivation before the number, company aside after the physics, unclear antecedent removed; NODAR claim tagged as a vendor claim, as elsewhere in the report."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-032",
