  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-039",
   "finding": "report #23",
   "old": "  - *Super-resolution* locates an isolated bright scatterer to about beamwidth ÷ $\\sqrt{2\\,{\\rm SNR}}$, where SNR is that scatterer's signal-to-noise power ratio. That is one-fourteenth of a beam at 20 dB (SNR = 100, and $\\sqrt{2\\times100}\\approx14$). It sharpens *where* a single point is, but it neither splits a dense surface into pixels nor creates returns from panels that reflect the signal away.",
   "new": "  - *Sub-beamwidth angle estimation* (sometimes loosely called super-resolution) locates an isolated bright scatterer to about beamwidth ÷ $\\sqrt{2\\,{\\rm SNR}}$, where SNR is that scatterer's signal-to-noise power ratio. That is one-fourteenth of a beam at 20 dB (SNR = 100, and $\\sqrt{2\\times100}\\approx14$). It sharpens *where* a single point is, but it does not separate two scatterers inside one beam, split a dense surface into pixels, or create returns from panels that reflect the signal away.",
   "why": "In radar usage super-resolution means separating two targets in one beam; this is single-target angle precision, and the rewrite says it cannot separate two."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-040",
   "finding": "report #22",
   "old": "about 3,000–6,000 angular cells. That matches a 2014 16-line lidar vertically, is 2–3× coarser horizontally (0.4–0.5° steps against the VLP-16's 0.2°), and has 25–200 times fewer cells than current automotive lidar ($150{,}000/6{,}000=25$ for the AT128 against the ceiling, up to $600{,}000/3{,}000=200$ for the ATX against a Mobileye-class radar), and only strong reflectors return within those cells.",
   "new": "about 3,000–6,000 angular cells, and only strong reflectors return within those cells. Vertically that matches a 2014 16-line lidar; horizontally it is 2–3× coarser (0.4–0.5° steps against the VLP-16's 0.2°). In total it has 25–200 times fewer cells than current automotive lidar: $150{,}000/6{,}000=25$ for the AT128 against the practical ceiling, and $600{,}000/3{,}000=200$ for the ATX against a Mobileye-class radar.",
   "why": "Splits the section's key result, a 60-word sentence with four claims, into three sentences."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-041",
   "finding": "report #9; symbol scheme (a_b for braking deceleration; shared inputs)",
   "old": "At constant deceleration $a$ (m/s²), a car at speed $v$ (m/s) stops within\n\n  $$d_{\\rm brake}=\\frac{v^2}{2a} \\tag{159}$$\n\n  once the brakes are applied. At 130 km/h ($v=36.1$ m/s), braking alone takes $v^2/(2a)=36.1^2/(2\\times6)\\approx109$ m at 6 m/s² and $36.1^2/(2\\times5)\\approx130$ m at 5 m/s².",
   "new": "At constant braking deceleration $a_{\\rm b}$ (m/s²), a car at speed $v$ (m/s) stops within\n\n  $$d_{\\rm brake}=\\frac{v^2}{2a_{\\rm b}} \\tag{159}$$\n\n  once the brakes are applied. At 130 km/h ($v=36.1$ m/s), braking alone takes $v^2/(2a_{\\rm b})=36.1^2/(2\\times6)\\approx109$ m at an assumed 6 m/s² and $36.1^2/(2\\times5)\\approx130$ m at the 5 m/s² that UN R157 (the UN regulation for automated lane keeping) assumes for its detection-range table (§C10.6(g) and (k)). Both are gentler than the 0.7 $g$ (6.87 m/s²) of §A6.1, Eq. (11), which would give $36.1^2/(2\\times6.87)\\approx95$ m: 0.7 $g$ describes a firm stop on a dry road, whereas 5 m/s² is a regulation's planning value.",
   "why": "a_b per the symbol scheme (a is the aim angle in Part A); says where 5 m/s² comes from and why it differs from §A6's 0.7 g."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-042",
   "finding": "report #33",
   "old": "at 150 m a 0.1° lidar (0.1° $=1.745\\times10^{-3}$ rad) puts about 26 cm per pixel on the car ($150\\times1.745\\times10^{-3}\\approx0.26$ m) and separates it from both the gantry and the road.",
   "new": "at 150 m a lidar with 0.1–0.2° vertical steps (0.1° $=1.745\\times10^{-3}$ rad) puts a scan line every 26–52 cm on the car ($150\\times1.745\\times10^{-3}\\approx0.26$ m and $150\\times3.49\\times10^{-3}\\approx0.52$ m), enough to separate it from both the gantry, about 4 m higher, and the road.",
   "why": "The point is about elevation, where this section's lidars step 0.2°; 'pixel' was a camera term."
  },
