  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-053",
   "finding": "report §3 flags (metric names; lidar reach in fog)",
   "old": "*Reading the table.* The snow row gives K-Radar's 3D average precision (AP3D, 0–100; higher is better) for detectors using only that sensor, with the normal-weather value in brackets.",
   "new": "*Reading the table.* The snow / sleet row gives K-Radar's 3D average precision (3D AP, 0–100; higher is better; §E3) for detectors using only that sensor: each cell gives the heavy-snow value, then the sleet value, with the normal-weather value in parentheses. For the lidar's 0.6–1 V in dense fog, compare the measured ≈0.6 V of §A5, item 3 and the model of §C0 subsection 8, which gives about 0.5–0.7 V at 20–50 m visibility but only 0.13–0.3 V in lighter fog (200–1,000 m visibility).",
   "why": "Explains the x / y pairs, uses the table's actual parentheses, and ties the fog entry to §A5 and §C0."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-054",
   "finding": "report #27",
   "old": "The answers are that miles alone cannot prove a fatality rate, and redundancy helps only if channels fail independently.",
   "new": "The answers are that road miles can bound a fatality rate only after hundreds of millions of failure-free miles on one system version, and that redundancy helps only if channels fail independently.",
   "why": "Eq. (160) shows that 275 M failure-free miles do bound the rate; the old wording overstated the result."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-055",
   "finding": "report #27; report §3 flag (pointers to add)",
   "old": "- Waymo's 271 M **cumulative rider-only** miles (driven with no one in the driver's seat) are near that numerical threshold. This does not satisfy RAND's *failure-free* demonstration premise or establish safety across all operating domains.\n- Tesla's 1 M unsupervised miles imply",
   "new": "- Waymo's 271 M **cumulative rider-only** miles (driven with no one in the driver's seat; to Jun 2026, §E1) are near that numerical threshold. They still do not satisfy RAND's *failure-free* demonstration premise, which needs one unchanging system, with the constant fatality rate that Eq. (160) assumes, to drive all those miles without a fatality; Waymo's system was modified along the way (the recalls listed in §E1). Nor do they establish safety across all operating domains.\n- Tesla's 1 M unsupervised miles (to 3 Sep 2026, §E1) imply",
   "why": "Adds the §E1 sources of both mileage figures and says which part of RAND's premise the fleet miles miss."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-056",
   "finding": "report #24; report §1 Problem 3 (table lead-in)",
   "old": "The model assumes two identical channels, $p\\ll1$, and that two independent failures in the same hour are not detected and recovered from in between; that last assumption is what lets the square of a per-hour probability be read again as a per-hour figure.",
   "new": "The model assumes two identical channels and $p\\ll1$. Its second term is the probability that both channels fail independently within the same hour, so it treats a failed channel as staying failed, unnoticed, for the rest of that hour. If failures are detected and the car reaches a safe state sooner, that term shrinks in proportion to the time a failure goes unnoticed. For $p=10^{-4}$/h, Eq. (161) gives these system failure rates:",
   "why": "Plain statement of the model's key assumption, plus a lead-in for the β table."
  },
  {
   "id": "01_A3-A6_E2-E5-057",
   "finding": "report #37",
   "old": "- Different physics lowers $\\beta$, because a condition that blinds one sensor leaves the other working: camera vs radar for weather, camera vs lidar for darkness and geometry.",
   "new": "- Different physics lowers the environmental part of $\\beta$, because a condition that blinds one sensor leaves the other working: camera vs radar for weather, camera vs lidar for darkness and geometry. Shared software, fusion logic, power and mounting remain common causes that sensor diversity does not remove.",
   "why": "Sensor diversity does not remove the shared software or mounting causes listed in the model's definition."
  },
